  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>The care environment carries duties under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and its associated regulations</li><li>Falls, and moving and handling of residents, are the commonest sources of harm and the core of any care safety audit</li><li>Hoists, slings and other lifting equipment need thorough examination under LOLER 1998; the audit checks it is in date</li><li>Premises risks include legionella on the water system and fire under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005</li><li>The audit does not make the home safe; acting on the findings does, and the registered manager owns the actions</li></ul></div><h2>What a care home health and safety audit is for</h2><p>A care home is a workplace and a home at once, full of ordinary hazards that hurt frail people fastest. A health and safety audit walks the whole environment, records the state of each control against the home's risk assessments, and turns what it finds into a fix. It carries duties under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and the regulations under it.</p><p>The audit does not make the home safe. Acting on the findings does. What the audit does is find the loose handrail, the overdue hoist check and the propped fire door while there is still time, and evidence each with a photo so the record holds up to an inspector, an insurer or a coroner.</p><h2>Falls, and moving and handling</h2><p>The commonest sources of serious harm in a care setting are falls, and moving and handling of residents. A safety walk gives most of its attention to these: flooring, lighting, handrails, clutter and trip hazards, call bells within reach, and the safe handling practice and equipment used to move people. Each is recorded with a photo where it helps, so a finding is evidenced rather than asserted.</p><h2>Hoists, slings and LOLER 1998</h2><p>Hoists, stand-aids, slings and other equipment that lifts residents are covered by the Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment Regulations 1998 (LOLER). Equipment that lifts people must have a thorough examination at set intervals by a competent person, typically every six months. The audit checks that each hoist and sling has a current examination, that slings are the right type and undamaged, and that staff use them safely. Quickler records the examination status and a photo of the certificate at the point of check; it does not perform the examination.</p><h2>Legionella, fire and the water system</h2><p>Legionella: care homes have vulnerable residents and complex water systems, so a written scheme of control, temperature monitoring and flushing of little-used outlets need checking against the legionella risk assessment. Fire: under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 the home is a regulated premises with a responsible person and a fire risk assessment, and controls to check: fire doors, alarms, escape routes, emergency lighting and evacuation arrangements. The audit records the state of each control; the specialist risk assessments stay the competent assessor's work. Check the current HSE and fire safety guidance, and treat this as general information, not legal advice.</p><h2>What to look for in a care safety audit tool</h2><ul><li><strong>On-the-floor completion:</strong> the walk recorded as it happens, not written up that evening</li><li><strong>Photo evidence per finding:</strong> the blocked exit or overdue label captured with a location and a timestamp</li><li><strong>Statutory-check tracking:</strong> LOLER examination dates and fire and legionella controls flagged when overdue</li><li><strong>Action list out of the box:</strong> each finding becomes an action with an owner and a date</li><li><strong>Right scope:</strong> the tool audits the environment; it is not a care record and no resident data is entered</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler records the safety walk and the evidence and turns it into an action list. It does not make the home safe, it does not perform LOLER examinations or fire and legionella risk assessments, and it is not a care record. The registered manager acting on the findings carries the safety. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What does a care home health and safety audit cover?</h3><p>It walks the whole care environment: falls hazards (flooring, lighting, handrails, clutter), safe moving and handling of residents, lifting equipment such as hoists and slings under LOLER 1998, legionella management on the water system, and fire precautions under the Fire Safety Order 2005. Each control is checked against the home's own risk assessments and recorded with evidence.</p><h3>How often should a care home run a safety audit?</h3><p>There is no single statutory frequency for an internal audit; it depends on your risk assessments and policies, with many homes running a monthly or quarterly environment walk alongside daily and weekly checks. Statutory examinations, such as LOLER thorough examination of hoists, have their own set intervals. Follow your own policy and the current HSE guidance.</p><h3>Does Quickler check the hoists and water system itself?</h3><p>No. Quickler records whether the LOLER examination is in date and the legionella and fire controls are in place, as evidence captured on the walk, with photos. It does not perform the thorough examination, the legionella risk assessment or the fire risk assessment. Those stay the competent assessor's work. Quickler is the audit and evidence layer around them.</p><h3>Can I run a care home safety walk over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The auditor receives each prompt in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the report and action list generate automatically. No separate app or login is required, and no resident care data is entered, because quickler is not a clinical record.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-audit-software-uk.html\">Care home audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/cqc-compliance-audit-checklist-uk.html\">CQC compliance audit checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/ipc-audit-care-home-uk.html\">IPC audit for care homes</a></li></ul>",
